We are given a system of linear equations:
   \begin{align*}
   x + y = 10 \quad &(1) \\
   2x + y = 13 \quad &(2)
   \end{align*}

To solve for $x$ and $y$, subtract (1) from (2):
   \begin{align*}
   (2x + y) - (x + y) = 13 - 10 \\
   2x - x + y - y = 3 \\
   x = 3
   \end{align*}

Substitute $x = 3$ into (1) to find $y$:
   \begin{align*}
   3 + y = 10 \\
   y = 7
   \end{align*}

Now that we have $x = 3$ and $y = 7$, we can find $x^2 - y^2$:
   \begin{align*}
   x^2 - y^2 = 3^2 - 7^2 \\
   = 9 - 49 \\
   = -40 \\
   \end{align*}

The final answer is:
   \begin{align*}
   x^2 - y^2 = \boxed{-40}
   \end{align*}

