We are given the probability of getting rain on any given day is $\frac{1}{10}$. The probability that it does not rain is $1 - \frac{1}{10} = \frac{9}{10}$.

Since there are 30 days in June, we can model this using a binomial distribution with $n = 30$ and $p = \frac{1}{10}$.

The probability that it rains on at most 2 days is the sum of the probabilities that it rains on 0, 1, or 2 days. This is:
   \begin{align*}
   \binom{30}{0} (\frac{1}{10})^0 (\frac{9}{10})^{30} + \binom{30}{1} (\frac{1}{10})^1 (\frac{9}{10})^{29} + \binom{30}{2} (\frac{1}{10})^2 (\frac{9}{10})^{28}
   \end{align*}

We will compute each term:
   \begin{align*}
   \binom{30}{0} (\frac{1}{10})^0 (\frac{9}{10})^{30} &\approx 0.0424 \\
   \binom{30}{1} (\frac{1}{10})^1 (\frac{9}{10})^{29} &\approx 0.1436 \\
   \binom{30}{2} (\frac{1}{10})^2 (\frac{9}{10})^{28} &\approx 0.2246
   \end{align*}

Adding them up gives:
   \begin{align*}
   0.0424 + 0.1436 + 0.2246 &\approx 0.4106
   \end{align*}

So the answer rounded to the nearest thousandth is:
   \begin{align*}
